 63% of  people are what they called sleepwalking  through their works you don't like it  you don't hate it 24% of people hate  their work so you think about that 87%  of people don't like the thing they're  doing most of the time I was like okay  what's what's going on here so it could  not be affecting our emotional and  mental health I discovered an amazing  Australian social scientists have done  the best research of this in the 1970s  guy called Michael Marmot who I got to  know see I can tell you how if you want  to give you the headline he discovered  the key factor not the only one but the  key factor that causes depression at  work not just not just unhappiness  depression actually even makes you more  likely to have a stress-related heart  attack if you go to work and you feel  you have no or little control  so you're just told what to do you are  significantly more likely to become  depressed and I think that's because we  all have an innate named the meaning in  our lives right and if you're controlled  all the time it disrupts your ability to  create meaning now that is a cause of  depression so I started thinking about  what would an antidepressant for that  look like so I went to Baltimore mmm I  met an amazing person called Meredith  Keough so Meredith and I think it's  really connects with a lot of things you  cover on your show so brilliantly David  Meredith used to go to bed every Sunday  like just sit with anxiety she had an  office job it wasn't the worst doctor's  job in the world she wasn't being  bullied or harassed but she just  couldn't bear the thought this was gonna  be her life that's four years and one  day with her husband Josh she did this  quite bold thing Josh had worked in bike  stores since he was a teenager and one  day you know it's insecure controlled  work as you can imagine one day with his  friends I mean the bikes Len they were  like what does that boss actually do  they quite liked their boss it was at  the bad guy but they thought well we fix  all the banks they didn't like this  experience of being boss too they  decided to do an experiment  they opened a rival bike store it's  called Baltimore bicycle works and it  doesn't work like most workplaces it's  but it's a democratic cooperative they  don't have a boss they take all the  decisions together big decisions they  share out the good tasks and the bad  tasso no one gets stuck with only the  bad tasks they obviously share the  income and what in this completely fits  professor marmots findings well this was  so fascinating to me is how many of them  talked about having been depressed and  anxious before and said they weren't  depressed and anxious in this different  environment that's important to say it's  not like they quit their jobs fixing  bikes and went off to become Beyonce's  backing singers right they fixed bikes  before they fix bikes now well they  change was the thing that causes  depression and anxiety which is being  controlled now I would argue that is an  antidepressant and as Josh put it that  innocence where I think it connects with  so much of the work you do on  corporations there is no reason why any  workplace should be structured in this a  depressogenic controlled way every  workplace could be a democratic work  very recently that the modern  corporation in its current form in human  history came into existence imagine how  many people you know who feel terrible  at the moment it would feel differently  if they knew that tomorrow they were  going into a workplace that they  controlled with their colleagues where  they could use their creativity and set  to set the priorities for the workplace  where there has to be a boss he's  elected and accountable to them that's a  completely different way of living and I  think a lot of that 87% of people in our  culture who dread or don't like the  thing they're doing most of the time  would start to feel differently about it  so in terms of so that's a very  interesting sort of situational  antidepressant as far as the more  traditional so-called treatments what do  you think is the role of antidepressants  typically the SSRI family not for  everybody but for some short term or  long term psychotherapy and of course  there's different modalities of that and  we could sort of assess the value of one  versus another in treating depression  what what's your sense of the different  sort of typical treatments that are out  there one of the things that I was  learning intellectually about these nine  causes of depression or anxiety and I  was kind of taking it on board and it  really kind of emotionally locked into  place for me when when I met this  amazing South African psychiatrist  called Derrick Sommerfeld and Derrick  happened to be in Cambodia when chemical  antidepressants were first introduced  there and the doctors there the  Cambodian doctors didn't know what it  worked what they were right so Derrick  explained and they said I wouldn't need  them we've already got antidepressants  and he said what do you mean he thought  I'm going to talk about like a herb or  remedy they told him a story there was a  farmer in their community who one day  had stood on a landmine from the  American invasion of Southeast Asia  and his leg had been blown off and they  gave him an artificial limb and he went  back to work in the rice fields but  apparently it's very painful to work  with an artificial bin in water  I imagine it was quite traumatic cousins  back in the fields we've been blown up  he starts to cry all day  classic depression and they said to  Derrick well we gave him an  antidepressant he said what did you do  they said well we were explained they  went and sat with him they listened to  his problems they realized that his pain  made sense it wasn't a pathology and  they figured well if we bought in Macau  we could become a dairy farmer and you  know then he wouldn't have to be in this  situation so they brought in an account  within a few weeks he stopped crying in  his depression when they said to Derrick  so you see doctor that care was an  antidepressant so I looked at lots of  places all over the world where they  have tried to pick up these insights and  and I found that they certainly placed a  chemical antidepressants 1.8 points on  the Hamilton scale is not zero points on  the Hamilton scale i well understand why  lots of people choose that option and  why it outweighs some of the really  significant side effects for a lot of  people therapy is great I've had a  therapist for 12 years I'm strongly in  favor of it I have a chapter about it in  the book but the most impressive  response is that I found were ones that  built on this insight about not trying  to blunt the symptoms but trying to deal  with the causes I'll give you one more  quick example as a doctor in East London  one of the heroes of my book lost  connections called Sam ever Anton so Sam  is a doctorate poor part of East London  actually where I lived for a long time  but they sadly was never my doctor and  Sam was really uncomfortable because  lots of people would come to him who  were depressed and anxious and he would  listen to their lives and he think we'll  of course you depressed I'm anxious  you're right to be depressed or anxious  you're being treated really badly you're  really lonely for example and but he'd  been taught in medical school even  though he actually knew the science was  much more sophisticated to say to people  who just got a chemical imbalance  we'll just drug you like me he's not  against the drugs he just thought this  is not enough so he tried an experiment  Sam I give you an example one of his  patients a woman called Lisa Cunningham  came to see him well I got to know Lisa  had been shut away in her home crippled  with depression and anxiety for seven  seven years and Lisa came to see Sam and  he said to our giving the drugs don't  worry I'm going to prescribe you  something else I'm gonna prescribe for  you to take part in a group there was an  area behind the doctors surgery that's  just kind of scrubland  and said we're going to support you I  want you to come a couple of times a  week and meet with other people here  other depressed and anxious people and I  want you just to turn this into  something beautiful Lisa was physically  sick with anxiety when she turned up to  that first one as time went on as the  weeks and months went by they  taught themselves gardening they have  got their fingers in the soil they had  same to talk about that wasn't how bad  they felt as human beings do when they  get together in groups they started to  solve each other's problems you know one  of the people in the group was sleeping  on the bus everyone else the public bus  everyone else was outraged they they  they started lobbying the local  authorities to get him a apartment which  succeeded they'd done something to  someone else which made them feel great  as Lisa put it to me as the flowers  began to bloom we began to believe there  was a study in Norway that found this  approach which is called social  prescribing is more than twice as  effective as chemical antidepressants I  think is it's dealing with the deep  under light one of the deep underlying  reasons why people feel so bad if you  have a human society that is you know  increasingly socially isolated when  people are cut off and told to think of  themselves just as economic maximizes  but people are constantly being told  that life is about money and status  which there's very strong proof makes  you depressed where people who make this  just think like this about screaming at  each other through Springs and then you  tell people the reason you feel about is  just cause you're biologically broken is  gonna create an environment where what  we've been doing it for 30 years and we  can see the results every year we've  done it depression and anxiety have  risen and if we carry on with this  approach it will carry on riseling that  the work this is not some wacky view  from me right is the Pacific World  Health Organization right  World Health Organization explained in  2011 mental health is produced socially  it is a social indicator above all else  it requires social solutions as well as  individual solutions the United Nations  in their official statement last year  for World Health Day said we needed to  talk less about chemical imbalances more  about power imbalances the kind of thing  that you cover every day sure yeah and  there is no question that many of the  elements of Western modern society that  have become sort of status quo and and  sort of the unquestionable elements of  it are definitely too connected to a lot  of what it is that that were identifying  we've been speaking with Johann Hari  he's author of the book lost connections  uncovering the real causes of depression  and the unexpected solutions Johan  thanks so much for talking to us about  this any more information they  to find out what Glenn Greenwald Naomi  Klein Hillary Clinton Elton John has  said about the book then go to WWE lost  connections calm they can hear audio  with loads of the scientists we've been  talking about and they can take a quiz  to see how much they know about the root  causes of depression anxiety and David  thank you for all the work you do every  day it's so important and exciting thank  you
 

